1.1.text:
Article 8

1. Social protection for women and children is a joint obligation among the Regional Government, the community, social organizations, and the business sector, implemented in an integrated and sustainable manner.
2. Social protection for women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) is prioritized for women and children who have lives that are not humanely decent and have the following social problem criteria: a. poverty b. neglect;
c. disability d. social dysfunction and behavioral deviation; and/or e. victims of gender-based violence and children and/or mistreatment, as well as victims of human trafficking;
f. victims of social conflict.
3. Social protection for women and children, as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented through: a. prevention efforts;
b. handling efforts;
c. recovery efforts;
d. protection and support efforts.

Article 3

Women and children with social problems as referred to in Article 2, include:
a. Neglected children;
b. Children who are victims of gender-based violence and/or are mistreated;
c. Delinquent children;
d. Street children;
e. Children with disabilities;
f. Women vulnerable to socio-economic conditions;
g. Women who are victims of gender-based violence and/or are mistreated;
h. women and children victims of human traffickingi. children with special needsj. children in emergency situations

Article 16

1. The Regional Government and/or the community provides social protection and the administration of social welfare to women who are unable, neglected, exploited, trafficked, and prostitutes.
2. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for women as referred to in paragraph (1), through: a. education and training services;
b. opportunities to work and do business;
c. physical, religious, mental, and social guidance;
d. health services;
e. legal assistance;
f. other special social protection.
3. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for women as referred to in paragraph (2) is the responsibility of SKPD/UKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.
4. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for women as referred to in paragraph (2), can be organized by the community, social organizations, and the business sector.

Article 22

1. Social protection and social welfare administration for women and children shall be the responsibility of the Regional Government.
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented through:
a. planning, implementation, development, guidance and supervision of social protection and social welfare administration for women and children;
b. application of service standards for social protection and social welfare administration for women and children;
c. provision and/or facilitation as well as facilities and infrastructure;
d. facilitation of social protection and social welfare administration for women and children;
e. development of institutional capacity and social resources in accordance with the development of science and technology;
f. facilitating participation from the community, social organizations and/or the business sector in social protection and social welfare administration for women and children.
3. The responsibility as referred to in paragraph (2) may be delegated to SKPD/UKPD related to social protection and social welfare administration for women and children.

CHAPTER III

PRINCIPLES, FUNCTIONS AND OBJECTIVES

Article 5

Social protection for women and children is based on:
a. humanity;
b. Justice and gender equality;
c. protection;
d. best interests of women and children;
e. non-discrimination;
f. participation;
g. professional; andh. partnership.

Article 24

1. The community may participate in social protection and social welfare administration for women and children.
2. The role as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by:
a. Individuals;
b. Families;
c. Religious organizations;
d. Social community organizations;
e. Non-governmental organizations;
f. Professional organizations;
g. Business entities;
h. Social welfare institutions; and
i. Foreign social welfare institutions.

Article 28

1. The community may establish a social organization to optimize participation in coordinating social protection and social welfare administration for women and children.
2. The regional government shall facilitate the establishment of social organizations as referred to in paragraph (1) according to applicable laws and regulations.
3. The social organization as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be established at the regency level and may have representatives in each sub-district and/or village.
4. The social organization as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be open, autonomous and not an institution having a hierarchical relationship with the regional government structure.
5. The social organization as referred to in paragraph (1) must meet the requirements:
a. non-profit seeking;
b. institutional in form;
c. oriented to the public interest;
d. managed professionally;
6. The social organization as referred to in paragraph (1) must obtain a permit from the Regent through the head of SKPD/UKPD responsible in the field of social protection and social welfare administration for women and children.
7. Further provisions regarding the licensing procedures as referred to in paragraph (6) shall refer to applicable laws and regulations.

Article 30

The Regional Government shall provide guidance for social protection and social welfare administration for women and children, including:

a. Coordination;
b. Establishment of guidelines and standards;
c. Provision of counseling, guidance, supervision, and consultation;
d. Research, monitoring, and evaluation.

Article 4

Social protection for women and children in the form of: a. Social assistance;
b. Social advocacy; and/orc. legal aid.

